<p data-start=3D"647" data-end=3D"696">This always causes <strong data-star=
t=3D"666" data-end=3D"687">shape drift by =C2=B11</strong> because:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"698" data-end=3D"800">
<li data-start=3D"698" data-end=3D"714">
<p data-start=3D"700" data-end=3D"714">721 is <strong data-start=3D"707" da=
ta-end=3D"714">odd</strong></p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"715" data-end=3D"756">
<p data-start=3D"717" data-end=3D"756">Striding with 2 creates rounding iss=
ues</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"757" data-end=3D"800">
<p data-start=3D"759" data-end=3D"800">Upsampling reconstructs <strong data=
-start=3D"783" data-end=3D"790">722</strong> sometimes</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"802" data-end=3D"842">This is a classic U-Net padding prob=
lem.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"844" data-end=3D"847">
<h1 data-start=3D"849" data-end=3D"892">=F0=9F=9B=A0=EF=B8=8F To fix this, =
you have 3 safe options:</h1>
<hr data-start=3D"894" data-end=3D"897">
<h1 data-start=3D"899" data-end=3D"948">=E2=9C=94 <strong data-start=3D"903=
" data-end=3D"948">OPTION 1 =E2=80=94 Crop latitude by 1 (very easy)</stron=
g></h1>
<p data-start=3D"949" data-end=3D"1017">We simply drop 1 latitude row from =
the ERA5 dataset before training.</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"1019" data-end=3D"1036">Why it works:</h3>
<ul data-start=3D"1037" data-end=3D"1209">
<li data-start=3D"1037" data-end=3D"1096">
<p data-start=3D"1039" data-end=3D"1096">CNNs don't care if size is 720=C3=
=971440 instead of 721=C3=971440</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1097" data-end=3D"1118">
<p data-start=3D"1099" data-end=3D"1118">Fixes shape drift</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1119" data-end=3D"1141">
<p data-start=3D"1121" data-end=3D"1141">Very safe and fast</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1142" data-end=3D"1209">
<p data-start=3D"1144" data-end=3D"1209">Almost all meteorology ML papers c=
rop 1 grid line for consistency</p>
</li>
</ul>
